el a bit  off so i put a number of hours into each  game  crossing back and forth between the two  but my initial thoughts stayed the same  i remember when i first tried out the  division 2 the character movement felt a  little heavy in comparison but as you'd  expect  you get used to it pretty quick the idea  behind this change was to allow for a  more realistic feel  the character is carrying a lot of gear  after all fortunately  unlike some other games i can think of  they don't overdo it  going back to the division 1 you do feel  a little more free  for lack of a better word the character  is light on their feet and super  responsive  but overall the movement in general  especially in and out of cover  feels a little clunky in comparison not  game breaking  just not as refined as the sequel  however where the movement went in a  more realistic direction  the weapon handling did the opposite the  division one has considerably more  weapon recoil  and i quite like this it provided a  greater point of difference between the  weapon archetypes  and it gave me more to consider when  playing around with builds  it also meant that attributes like  handling and stability actually mattered  especially on console however this also  provided more of a skill gap  between new players and those who had  learnt over time to compensate for the  recoil  which is why i think they heavily  reduced it for the casual player  weapons being too difficult to handle  becomes a quality of life issue  it also reduced the number of viable  weapons on console for the average  player  and then we have the infamous bullet  sponge issue that a lot of people hated  about the game  this is a luda shooter and a large part  of the game is around collecting loot  and creating builds that work for your  style of play if every enemy could be  killed with a single bullet to the head  the need for builds have effectively  been removed  but stepping back from the division 2  into the original i had completely  forgotten how many more bullets the  enemies needed to be taken down  i can now appreciate the balance that  has been put into the division too  builds still matter but the combat isn't  so sluggish  this alone has made the game feel much  faster paced than division one  probably the biggest letdown people have  with the division two is the atmosphere  and i get it the division one's dark  snowy streets with its tall buildings  looming overhead  really added to the impact of what had  happened in the story it felt lonely  cold deserted and is entirely fitting  for a lawless post-apocalyptic city  and all the hopeful twinkling christmas  lights and decorations  only added to the somber reality of a  christmas that would never occur  then there is the division two while  they did add some of the tall buildings  and dirty streets  with the addition of warlords of new  york it just doesn't have the same  gritty feeling and total immersion of  the original  it's sunny agents are running around in  shorts tank tops  and jandals or flip-flops or thongs  depending on what part of the world  you're from  but if you consider the direction  they've taken with the story it makes  complete sense  in the division one we're operating  around a city only weeks after the  outbreak  discovering what has really happened and  who was responsible  in the division two seven months have  passed we've been through the worst of  the virus  and it's now about helping those who  have survived thus far in  re-establishing order  so to be fair the dark and snowy streets  of new york probably wouldn't have fit  into this change of narrative quite so  well  however with this new storyline that  takes place in washington dc  although not as immersive it's  considerably more detailed than  manhattan  and on top of this there is a lot more  going on this environment just feels  more alive  i'm someone who enjoys open worlds and a  large part of this is because of the  option to explore  to find hidden gems spotted around the  map to watch the npcs go about their  business  the best example of this being ghost  recon although i've enjoyed both games  wildlands just feels more natural and  like there is more going on  it's more realistic and rough whereas  breakpoint  just feels a little dead plus the open  world activities only add to this  rescuing civilians capturing control  points ambushing supply routes  it's made roaming the streets and  activity on its own  the division one had years of balancing  issues with the current meta at the time  and that would change every patch or so  would be the only build worth running  it wasn't just a case of being slightly  better the meta builds would often  outclass everything else in every way it  wasn't until about 1.7 from memory  that the build diversity was in its best  place there were still some meta builds  but they weren't as overpowered as the  previous ones  this was the time of the ninja bike  messenger builds and going back i  actually think i started creating  content around  this time unfortunately this was rather  short-lived  as 1.8 and beyond brought classified  sets to the table  i admit i myself did like the more class  style of play that these gave the game  but i didn't really like how the  classified sets became the only thing to  run  other builds could work but the  classified bonuses were just too  powerful  and so being creative with builds  stopped being a thing  with the introduction of classified  pieces the game also became unrewarding  as the most efficient way of farming  these was through the global events  so in its current form i rate the  division 2 far higher when it comes to  build diversity and post-game  progression  as you make your way through the levels  and eventually get to level 40 you can  start putting together proper  in-game builds immediately where the  division 1 locks you into waiting on  global events  to acquire the best gear  like i said earlier i'm not really a  huge fan of division pvp  previously i used to spend a little bit  of time in the last stand but it was  hardly what i'd call  a competitive experience it was more for  just a bit of fun  as a break from doing other things  however with the division two  it isn't even just a bit of fun it's  just horrible  while the balance of pve is in an okay  place right now the pvp  is pretty much broken unless you're  running one of the two to three relevant  builds in there  you aren't going to be having a good  time to be fair the build variety wasn't  particularly amazing in the division one  either  but at least with game modes like last  stand you could join in in the control  chaos  and have some fun just based on the  number of people alone  incoming unpopular opinion i don't think  the division franchise was ever made  with pvp in mind  of it being one of the main activities  in the game and this idea is only  further supported by what they did with  it in the division two  early on in the game's life cycle i  spent well over a thousand hours roaming  around the division one dark zone  not in a group i was a solo player 95 of  the time  i found it was one of the best places to  farm as you had a greater chance of  finding high-end gear  and set items but there was also always  a risk of having them taken from you by  other agents  the dark zone was one of the first  things that grabbed my attention when  reading about the up-and-coming game  a high-risk high-reward pve vp area  i was more than happy to take the risk  and admittedly it used to scare the crap  out of me when i first started running  it  but over time the developers eventually  conceded to the popular opinion across  the community  and made it so that the dart zone was no  longer the most efficient place to farm  effectively removing the incentive for  going there in the first place  from then on the bulk of the people who  entered the cereal were groups of four  looking for pvp  and of course i keep going in because i  like the challenge of playing as a solo  player  either playing stealth or watching teams  of four coming across me thinking i was  an easy target  however i eventually changed over to pc  and without the reward  i'd just never bother going back and the  division 2 dark zones continued on with  the same issue  except it got even worse at first i  actually liked the idea of the smaller  dark zones  increasing the chance of coming across  other players but it ended up changing  the whole feel of the dark zone  after 20 minutes of running around you  realize how small these new dark zones  really are  completely inappropriate for solo  players stealth was never going to be an  option  and again without any sort of incentive  to go in there they remain fairly unused  this would have to be one of the most  important parts for this genre of game  for these mini mmo style games it's  important to have something to build  towards  well after the campaign has been  completed with the division 1 we have  incursions pvp dark zone underground  survival resistance and legendary  missions  the division 2 has raids pvp dark zone  summit open world activities legendary  missions and seasons  let's take away pvp in the dark zone  because we've already talked about that  underground survival and resistance are  all completely separate dedicated game  modes  the only thing that the division 2 has  that could slightly resemble any of  these  is summit but this fell well short of  the mark  the summit feels like it was a rush game  mode that was quickly put together to  silence some of the complaints that were  happening at the time  and more importantly from a story point  of view we're not even given a reason as  to why we're climbing that building  incursions were intended to be mini  raids or long missions  but it didn't take long before people  were able to speed run these  in the division 2 we got full raids  these were a nice addition that a large  amount 